## Binary Search on Answer Space

### The Pattern
Whenever a problem asks for the **minimum possible maximum** or **maximum possible minimum** with a monotonic condition (if $X$ is valid, any $Y > X$ is also valid), apply Binary Search on the **answer range** $[\text{min}, \text{max}]$ rather than searching indices of an array!

### Classic Problem: Koko Eating Bananas (LeetCode 875)
*Problem*: Koko loves bananas. There are $n$ piles. Find minimum speed $k$ to eat all piles within $h$ hours.

```java
public int minEatingSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {
    int low = 1;
    int high = Arrays.stream(piles).max().getAsInt();
    int ans = high;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (canEatInTime(piles, mid, h)) {
            ans = mid;         // Mid is viable, try to find an even smaller speed
            high = mid - 1;
        } else {
            low = mid + 1;     // Too slow, increase speed
        }
    }
    return ans;
}

private boolean canEatInTime(int[] piles, int speed, int maxHours) {
    long hoursSpent = 0;
    for (int pile : piles) {
        hoursSpent += (pile + speed - 1) / speed; // Ceiling division without floating point
    }
    return hoursSpent <= maxHours;
}
```

### Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: $O(N \log(\max(\text{piles})))$ where $N$ is array length.
- **Space**: $O(1)$ constant memory.

## Sliding Window: Dynamic Window Sizing

### The Algorithm
Maintain a sliding window $[\text{left}, \text{right}]$ where all characters are guaranteed unique. Store the last seen index of each character in an array/map:

```java
public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
    int[] lastSeen = new int[128];
    Arrays.fill(lastSeen, -1);

    int maxLength = 0;
    int left = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
        char c = s.charAt(right);

        // If character seen inside current window, jump left pointer forward:
        if (lastSeen[c] >= left) {
            left = lastSeen[c] + 1;
        }

        lastSeen[c] = right;
        maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1);
    }

    return maxLength;
}
```

### Complexity
- **Time**: $O(N)$ — The `right` pointer traverses the string once; the `left` pointer jumps forward without rewinding.
- **Space**: $O(1)$ fixed 128-byte array for ASCII table.

## Topological Sort & Dependency Resolution

### What is a DAG?
A **Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)** represents dependencies with no circular loops. Topological sorting orders vertices such that for every directed edge $u \to v$, vertex $u$ comes before $v$.

### Kahn's Algorithm (BFS with In-Degree Array)

```java
public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
    List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
    int[] inDegree = new int[numCourses];

    // 1. Build adjacency list and compute in-degrees
    for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {
        adj.get(pre[1]).add(pre[0]);
        inDegree[pre[0]]++;
    }

    // 2. Add all nodes with 0 in-degree (no prerequisites) to queue
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {
        if (inDegree[i] == 0) queue.offer(i);
    }

    int[] order = new int[numCourses];
    int index = 0;

    // 3. Process nodes and decrement neighbor in-degrees
    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int curr = queue.poll();
        order[index++] = curr;

        for (int neighbor : adj.get(curr)) {
            inDegree[neighbor]--;
            if (inDegree[neighbor] == 0) {
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }

    // If cycle detected, not all courses can be finished:
    return index == numCourses ? order : new int[0];
}
```

### Complexity
- **Time**: $O(V + E)$ visiting every vertex and edge once.
- **Space**: $O(V + E)$ for adjacency list representation.

## Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm

### Greedy Choice Property
At each step, select the unvisited vertex with the minimum known distance from the source. Non-negative weights guarantee that this distance is final.

```java
public int[] dijkstra(int n, List<List<int[]>> adj, int src) {
    int[] dist = new int[n];
    Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
    dist[src] = 0;

    // Min-heap ordered by distance: int[] {node, distance}
    PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[1]));
    pq.offer(new int[]{src, 0});

    while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
        int[] curr = pq.poll();
        int u = curr[0];
        int d = curr[1];

        // Stale entry in heap (shorter path already found):
        if (d > dist[u]) continue;

        for (int[] edge : adj.get(u)) {
            int v = edge[0];
            int weight = edge[1];

            if (dist[u] + weight < dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + weight;
                pq.offer(new int[]{v, dist[v]});
            }
        }
    }
    return dist;
}
```

### Complexity
- **Time**: $O((V + E) \log V)$ using a binary min-heap.
- **Space**: $O(V + E)$ for graph representation and distance array.

## 0/1 Knapsack & Space Optimization

### The Recurrence Relation
For item $i$ with weight $w[i]$ and value $v[i]$ at remaining capacity $C$:

$$\text{dp}[i][C] = \max(\text{dp}[i-1][C],\; v[i] + \text{dp}[i-1][C - w[i]])$$

### Optimal 1D Space Solution ($O(C)$ memory)
Notice that row $i$ only reads from the previous row $i-1$. By iterating capacity in **reverse (from $W$ down to $w[i]$)**, we can compress the 2D table into a single 1D array without overwriting values needed in the current step!

```java
public int knapsack(int W, int[] wt, int[] val, int n) {
    int[] dp = new int[W + 1];

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // Iterate backwards to prevent using the same item multiple times:
        for (int c = W; c >= wt[i]; c--) {
            dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], val[i] + dp[c - wt[i]]);
        }
    }
    return dp[W];
}
```

> **Unbounded Knapsack vs 0/1 Knapsack:**
> - **0/1 Knapsack** (items used at most once): Loop capacity **backwards** ($W \to wt[i]$).
> - **Unbounded Knapsack** (items can be reused infinitely, e.g. Coin Change): Loop capacity **forwards** ($wt[i] \to W$).

## Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)

### Recurrence Relation
Given strings $S_1$ of length $m$ and $S_2$ of length $n$:
- If $S_1[i-1] == S_2[j-1]$: Characters match, $\text{dp}[i][j] = 1 + \text{dp}[i-1][j-1]$.
- If characters do not match: $\text{dp}[i][j] = \max(\text{dp}[i-1][j],\; \text{dp}[i][j-1])$.

```java
public int longestCommonSubsequence(String text1, String text2) {
    int m = text1.length();
    int n = text2.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (text1.charAt(i - 1) == text2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[m][n];
}
```

### Applications of LCS
- **Git Diff Algorithm**: Myers diff algorithm uses LCS to identify line additions and deletions.
- **Edit Distance (Levenshtein)**: Extends LCS by adding substitution cost ($1 + \text{dp}[i-1][j-1]$).
